wil6210: notice a firmware that restarts behind the host's back, and recover it

Under sustained TX load the firmware has been seen to restart itself without
reporting it: no assert code, no event - the TX ring stays stopped, counters
freeze, and WMI only starts timing out once something asks the firmware for
anything. By then resetting the device hangs the whole board and only a power
cycle brings it back, so the restart has to be caught while the chip is still
sane.

The restart is visible in the FW log ring header, which costs one read to
check: the per-module level bytes are written by the driver and nobody else,
so a firmware that came up fresh has all of them zeroed.

Poll them every fw_watchdog_ms and hand the device to the existing FW error
recovery path, the same one the assert interrupt uses. Needs the FW log armed
(module parameter or debugfs); 0, the default, keeps the watchdog off.

A write pointer that stands still is deliberately not taken as a hang: FW
6.2, and a 4.1 station before association, log nothing for seconds at a time,
and such a check reset healthy links every 6-18 s.
--- a/drivers/net/wireless/ath/wil6210/debugfs.c
